    <p class="eyebrow">How Sermon Steward works</p>
    <h1>We read your sermon the way a careful listener would.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Not by the paragraph. Not by the word count. By what you are doing in the pulpit at that moment — explaining the text, making a claim, telling a story, turning to your people. That is how the extras get written from the sermon you actually preached.</p>
  </section>

  <article class="step">
    <h2><span class="n">1</span> We cut where the sermon turns</h2>
    <p>A sermon is not a block of audio. It moves. You open the text. You press a truth. You reach for a picture. You look your people in the eye.</p>
    <p>We mark those turns. Each slice is a unit: a stretch of the sermon that is doing one job, until the job changes. That is why a Steward page does not feel like a transcript dumped under a player. It feels like the sermon, in order.</p>
    <p>See it on a live church: <a href="https://sermonsteward.com/ProvidenceLenexa/sermons/">Providence sermons</a> — click any sermon. The transcript is broken into those units.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="step">
    <h2><span class="n">2</span> We name what that slice is doing</h2>
    <p>Once the sermon is cut, we name the work of each slice — in ordinary words a pastor already uses.</p>
    <p>You will see this on a live page as type, tone, and method, plus a short map of what is in the sermon: the shape of the argument, the doctrines it touches, the Scriptures it cites, the illustrations, the claims.</p>
    <p>We are not grading you. We are labeling the moves so the rest of the page can be honest. Small-group questions should come from the places you applied the text. A family conversation should come from the claim you actually made, not from a generic theme we guessed.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="step">
    <h2><span class="n">3</span> The extras come out of those slices</h2>
    <p>For $30 a month you get the hosting — a clean page, the audio, an automatic transcript, browse by Scripture, series, and topic — and the resources for your people.</p>
    <p>Those resources are pulled from the units, not invented beside them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="tick">&check;</span> Daily readings from the Scriptures you opened</li>
      <li><span class="tick">&check;</span> Small-group questions from the claims and applications you made</li>
      <li><span class="tick">&check;</span> Family and couples conversation guides from the same sermon</li>
      <li><span class="tick">&check;</span> Cross-references that showed up in the preaching</li>
    </ul>
    <p>That is the point of the free try, too. Upload one MP3 at <a href="https://try.sermonsteward.com">https://try.sermonsteward.com</a>. No account. We email a report — usually in about fifteen minutes — drawn from that sermon. We use the file to make the report. We never publish it without your say-so.</p>
    <p>A try is a try. It does not become your church library.</p>
  </article>

